<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Newton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1807">1807</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Newton was an English evangelical Anglican cleric and abolitionist who had earlier been a captain of slave ships and an investor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. He went to sea young, was pressed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in 1743 and was for a time a servant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>; after a conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> he renounced the trade, was ordained and served as parish priest at Olney, Buckinghamshire, for two decades. With the poet William Cowper he published Olney Hymns in 1779, including the hymn now known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a>. His pamphlet Thoughts Upon the Slave Trade (1788) described the conditions of the Middle Passage, and he lived to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> abolish the slave trade in 1807.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-newton.jpg" alt="John Newton" width="220" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Contemporary portrait · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohnNewtonColour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Newton was born at Wapping, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 4 August 1725, the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> shipmaster. His mother died of tuberculosis in 1732. He first went to sea at 11 with his father and sailed six voyages. In 1743 he was pressed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> and became a midshipman aboard HMS Harwich. After he tried to desert he was flogged and reduced to common seaman.</p><p>He then moved to a slave ship, Pegasus, bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. In 1745 he was traded to a slaver and taken as a servant to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, a time he later called being a servant of slaves in West Africa. Early in 1748 a sea captain sent by his father rescued him, and in a severe storm off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a> during the voyage home he began to pray for mercy. He dated his conversion to 21 March 1748, though he later said that he was not a believer in the full sense until considerably afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Newton returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> in 1748 and became first mate of the slave ship Brownlow. He then made three voyages as captain, of the Duke of Argyle (1750) and the African (1752 to 1753 and 1753 to 1754). After a severe stroke in 1754 he gave up the sea but continued to invest in the slaving operations of Joseph Manesty. He married Mary Catlett on 12 February 1750. In 1755 he became tide surveyor at Liverpool and studied Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>.</p><p>He applied to be ordained in 1757 and was accepted only after more than seven years. He became deacon on 29 April 1764 and priest on 17 June, as curate of Olney in Buckinghamshire, where John Thornton helped to pay his stipend. His preaching drew so many that a gallery was added to the church. In 1779 he became rector of St Mary Woolnoth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Visitors who sought his advice included the writer Hannah More and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, whom he encouraged to stay in Parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The poet William Cowper moved to Olney in 1767 and worked with Newton on Olney Hymns, published in 1779. It included Glorious Things of Thee Are Spoken, How Sweet the Name of Jesus Sounds and Faith's Review and Expectation, which came to be known by its opening phrase. Newton also published an anonymous autobiography, An Authentic Narrative, in 1764.</p><p>In 1788 he broke a silence of 34 years with Thoughts Upon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a>, which described conditions on the slave ships in the Middle Passage. He called his part in the trade a subject of humiliating reflection and sent a copy to every Member of Parliament. He died on 21 December 1807, aged 82, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade Act</a> had passed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Newton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>John Newton</b> was born in Wapping, London, in 1725 and went to sea at eleven.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish priest Bernard Callan put it into Irish around 1800, <b>John Newton</b> made a loose Protestant version, and Ambrose Bierce a satirical one in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>In English hymnals the tune is known as &quot;Austria&quot; or &quot;Austrian Hymn&quot;, most often with <b>John Newton</b>'s &quot;Glorious Things of Thee Are Spoken&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>The curate <b>John Newton</b>, a former slave-ship captain, introduced the hymn later known as 'Amazing Grace' at a New Year's Day service in Olney, Buckinghamshire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1778" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Newton"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I hope it will always be a subject of humiliating reflection to me, that I was, once, an active instrument, in a business at which my heart now shudders.&#8221;</p><footer>Thoughts upon the African Slave Trade (1788) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/68056/pg68056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am bound, in conscience, to take shame to myself by a public confession, which, however sincere, comes too late to prevent, or repair, the misery and mischief to which I have, formerly, been accessary.&#8221;</p><footer>Thoughts upon the African Slave Trade (1788) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/68056/pg68056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is always taken for granted, that they will attempt to gain their liberty, if possible.&#8221;</p><footer>Thoughts upon the African Slave Trade (1788), on the captives in the ships &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/68056/pg68056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
